// Practice content. Data only.
// CARDS: model answers for the plan's self-check items (keyed by item id).
window.CARDS = {
  d1c6: "<b>Why three levels:</b> one data center can fail, and users far away from it wait longer. AWS therefore builds at three levels: Regions to choose where data lives, Availability Zones to survive the loss of a data center, and edge locations to serve users from nearby.<br><b>Region:</b> you choose it for data residency, latency to users, service availability and price. It is a separate geographic area with at least three AZs. Data stays in a Region unless you move it.<br><b>Availability Zone:</b> spread resources across at least two AZs for high availability. An AZ is one or more discrete data centers with independent power, cooling and physical security. It is kilometers away from the other AZs in its Region but joined to them by low-latency links.<br><b>Edge location:</b> serves users from nearby. It is a point of presence, and there are far more of them than Regions. CloudFront, Route 53, Global Accelerator and edge functions use them.<br><b>Pick:</b> Multi-AZ protects against a data-center failure. Multi-Region protects against a Regional outage or meets geographic rules.",

  d1c7: "<b>Why so many ways to pay:</b> the same server costs less when you commit to using it for years, or accept that AWS may take it back. Each option trades a discount for a commitment or a risk.<br><b>On-Demand:</b> for short, unpredictable or uninterruptible work. You pay per second or hour with no commitment.<br><b>Savings Plans:</b> you commit to a $/hour spend for 1 or 3 years. A Compute Savings Plan (up to 66% off) floats across families, Regions, Fargate and Lambda. An EC2 Instance Savings Plan (up to 72%) is tied to one family in one Region.<br><b>Reserved Instances:</b> a 1- or 3-year commitment to specific instance attributes. Standard gives up to 72% off, and Convertible can be exchanged. Only zonal RIs reserve capacity.<br><b>Spot:</b> only for fault-tolerant, flexible work. It is spare capacity at up to 90% off, reclaimed with a 2-minute warning.<br><b>Dedicated Hosts:</b> a whole physical server with socket and core visibility, for bring-your-own-license (BYOL) software. <b>Dedicated Instances</b> only guarantee no hardware sharing with other accounts.<br><b>Pick:</b> steady use for years: Savings Plans or Reserved Instances. Work that can be interrupted: Spot. Short or unpredictable work that cannot: On-Demand. Capacity with no term commitment: On-Demand Capacity Reservations.",

  d1c8: "<b>Why three ways to run code:</b> software differs. Some needs a whole server that you control. Some is a short task set off by an event. Some is already packaged as a container. The more AWS runs for you, the less you have to manage.<br><b>EC2:</b> for long-running or legacy software, licensed apps and lift-and-shift. You get full control of the OS, instance type and GPUs. In return you patch, scale and secure the instances.<br><b>Lambda:</b> ideal for spiky, short, glue-style tasks. Event-driven functions run up to 15 minutes per invocation with up to 10 GB of memory. They scale to zero and are billed per request and duration. Newer options run longer. Lambda Managed Instances allow 90 minutes for asynchronous invocations. Durable functions pause and resume a multi-step workflow for up to a year. Lambda MicroVMs live up to 8 hours. A single invocation of a standard function is still capped at 15 minutes.<br><b>ECS on Fargate:</b> containers without managing hosts. It fits long-running services, jobs longer than 15 minutes, and apps already packaged as containers.<br><b>Pick:</b> a runtime over 15 minutes rules out a standard Lambda function. OS access, special hardware or host-level agents point to EC2 (or ECS/EKS on EC2). Least operational overhead points to Lambda or Fargate.",

  d2c1: "<b>Why there are two:</b> a security group protects one instance, a network ACL a whole subnet. You can secure instances with security groups alone; a NACL adds a second layer in front of them.<br><b>Security group</b> (instance level): attached to an ENI/instance. Stateful: return traffic is allowed automatically. Allow rules only, all evaluated together. A rule can reference another security group. A new group denies all inbound and allows all outbound traffic.<br><b>Network ACL</b> (subnet level): stateless, so you must allow return traffic yourself, usually ephemeral ports 1024–65535. Allow and deny rules, evaluated in number order, and the first match wins. The default NACL allows everything; a new custom NACL denies everything until you add rules.<br><b>Pick:</b> to block a specific IP or CIDR, use a NACL deny rule, because security groups cannot deny.",

  d2c2: "<b>Why there are two:</b> VPCs are isolated from each other, and sooner or later some of them must talk privately. Peering joins one pair directly. A transit gateway is a hub, for when there are too many pairs to connect one by one.<br><b>VPC peering:</b> a one-to-one private connection between two VPCs, in the same or a different account or Region. It is not transitive: A–B plus B–C does not give A–C. CIDRs must not overlap. A VPC cannot use its peer's internet gateway, NAT gateway, VPN or Direct Connect. There is no hourly fee: you pay only for data that crosses AZs or Regions. That makes it cheapest for a few VPCs, but a full mesh needs n(n–1)/2 connections.<br><b>Transit Gateway:</b> a Regional hub-and-spoke router for many VPCs plus Site-to-Site VPN and Direct Connect. It routes transitively and uses multiple route tables for segmentation. It peers with Transit Gateways in other Regions and is shared across accounts with AWS RAM. It is billed per attachment-hour and per GB processed.<br><b>Pick:</b> a handful of VPCs: peering. Dozens of VPCs or hybrid connectivity: Transit Gateway.",

  d2c3: "<b>Why there are two:</b> instances in a private subnet must reach AWS services such as S3 without a path over the internet. A VPC endpoint is that private door. The gateway type is the simple, free one for two services. The interface type covers most services and can be reached from more places.<br><b>Gateway endpoint:</b> only for S3 and DynamoDB. It is added as a route-table target (a prefix list) and is free of charge. Only traffic that starts inside that VPC can use it: not traffic from on-premises networks, from peered VPCs in other Regions or through a transit gateway. Restrict it with an endpoint policy.<br><b>Interface endpoint (PrivateLink):</b> an ENI (network interface) with a private IP in your subnets. It is available for most AWS services, S3 and DynamoDB included, and for partner services or your own behind an NLB. It is protected by security groups and reachable over Direct Connect, VPN and peering. It is billed per AZ-hour plus per GB.<br><b>Pick:</b> private S3 access from EC2 at the lowest cost: gateway endpoint. Private S3 access from on-premises over Direct Connect: interface endpoint.",

  d3c1: "<b>Why four kinds of storage:</b> programs reach data in different ways: as a disk, as a shared folder, or as objects fetched through an API. Each way needs a different service, so the first question is who must reach the data and how.<br><b>EBS:</b> a disk for one instance. Block storage in one AZ (io1/io2 Multi-Attach is the exception, same AZ only). gp3 is general-purpose SSD with a 3,000 IOPS / 125 MiB/s baseline and performance set independently of size. io2 Block Express gives the highest IOPS and sub-millisecond latency for critical databases. st1 is throughput HDD for large sequential workloads. sc1 is the cheapest cold HDD. HDD volumes cannot be boot volumes.<br><b>EFS:</b> the same files on many Linux instances. A managed, elastic NFS file system, mounted by many instances across AZs at once.<br><b>FSx:</b> a managed file system of one specific kind. Windows File Server (SMB, Active Directory). Lustre (HPC/ML, linked to S3). NetApp ONTAP (NFS, SMB and iSCSI; NetApp migrations). OpenZFS (ZFS migrations).<br><b>S3:</b> highly durable object storage accessed by API, for static assets, data lakes, backups and archives. It is not a block device. The newer Amazon S3 Files feature can mount a bucket as an NFS file system, but S3 itself stores objects.<br><b>Pick:</b> a disk for one instance: EBS. The same files on many Linux instances: EFS. Windows, Lustre, ONTAP or OpenZFS named in the stem: FSx. Objects through an API: S3.",

  d3c2: "<b>Why there are two:</b> a database has two different problems. It can fail, and it can get more reads than it can serve. Multi-AZ answers the first and a read replica the second.<br><b>Multi-AZ (DB instance):</b> for availability, not performance. RDS replicates synchronously to a standby in another AZ. Failover is automatic, typically 1–2 minutes, and repoints the DNS endpoint. The standby serves no reads.<br><b>Read replica:</b> for read capacity. An asynchronous copy that serves reads, in the same AZ, another AZ or another Region (up to 15 per source instance for most engines). The app must send reads to it and tolerate replication lag. A replica can be promoted manually, so a cross-Region replica doubles as a simple DR option.<br><b>Both at once:</b> Multi-AZ DB cluster deployments add two readable standbys. Aurora replicas act as both read capacity and failover targets.<br><b>Pick:</b> stay up when an AZ or instance fails: Multi-AZ. Take reads off the primary: read replica.",

  d3c3: "<b>Why so many classes:</b> data is usually read less often as it ages. Storing rarely read data can cost less if you accept a retrieval fee, a minimum storage time or a wait. The classes let you choose that trade for each object.<br><b>Standard:</b> frequent access, milliseconds, at least 3 AZs.<br><b>Intelligent-Tiering:</b> unknown or changing access. It moves objects automatically for a small monitoring fee, with no retrieval fees.<br><b>Standard-IA:</b> infrequent but instant access. Per-GB retrieval fee, 30-day minimum.<br><b>One Zone-IA:</b> the same in a single AZ, only for re-creatable data.<br><b>Express One Zone:</b> single-digit-millisecond performance in one AZ.<br><b>Glacier Instant Retrieval:</b> milliseconds, for data read about once a quarter. 90-day minimum.<br><b>Glacier Flexible Retrieval:</b> expedited 1–5 minutes, standard 3–5 hours, bulk 5–12 hours. 90-day minimum.<br><b>Glacier Deep Archive:</b> lowest price. Standard within 12 hours, bulk within 48 hours. 180-day minimum.<br><b>Pick:</b> by how often the data is read and how long you can wait for it. Lifecycle rules move objects down the classes as they age.",

  d3c4: "<b>Why two pairs:</b> reading the same data from a database again and again can be too slow, so a cache in memory goes in front of it. Which cache you use depends on the database, and the database depends on how you query the data.<br><b>DynamoDB + DAX:</b> for key-based access at massive scale. DynamoDB is serverless NoSQL (key-value and document) with single-digit-millisecond latency at any scale. DAX is an in-memory, write-through cache made only for DynamoDB. It brings reads down to microseconds through an API-compatible client, so code changes are minimal.<br><b>RDS + ElastiCache:</b> for complex queries with hot reads. RDS gives relational SQL, joins and transactions with a defined schema. ElastiCache (Valkey, Redis OSS or Memcached) is a general-purpose in-memory cache in front of any data store. The application must implement the caching pattern itself: lazy loading, write-through, TTLs. ElastiCache also holds sessions and leaderboards.<br><b>Pick:</b> key-based access at massive scale: DynamoDB, plus DAX for microseconds. Complex queries or relational integrity with hot reads: RDS plus ElastiCache.",

  d4c1: "<b>Why two kinds of policy:</b> access can be decided from two sides. One side says what a person or role may do. The other says who may touch a certain resource. AWS has a policy type for each side.<br><b>Identity-based policy:</b> attaches to a user, group or role and states what that principal can do. It has no Principal element.<br><b>Resource-based policy:</b> attaches to a resource and names the Principal. Examples: S3 bucket policy, SQS or SNS policy, KMS key policy, Lambda resource policy. It can grant cross-account access without the caller switching roles.<br><b>Evaluation:</b> every request starts as an implicit deny, and an explicit Allow is needed. An explicit Deny in any applicable policy (SCP, RCP, permissions boundary, session policy, identity or resource policy) overrides every Allow.<br><b>Pick:</b> within one account an Allow in either the identity or the resource policy is usually enough. Across accounts both sides must allow.",

  d4c2: "<b>Why SCPs exist:</b> in an organization with many accounts, the administrators of each account could otherwise allow anything inside it. A service control policy (SCP) is a limit set from above, by AWS Organizations, that applies whatever the account's own policies say.<br><b>What it is:</b> an AWS Organizations guardrail attached to the root, OUs or accounts. It sets the <b>maximum</b> permissions for IAM users and roles in member accounts, including each member account's root user.<br><b>Can:</b> deny services or actions, restrict Regions (<code>aws:RequestedRegion</code>), stop accounts leaving the organization or disabling CloudTrail and GuardDuty, and require conditions such as tags or encryption.<br><b>Cannot:</b> grant permissions (an identity or resource policy must still allow), restrict the management account, or restrict service-linked roles. Effective permissions are the intersection of every SCP from the root down the OU path.<br><b>Pick:</b> a limit on your own principals in member accounts: an SCP. A cap on what principals outside your organization can do with your resources: a resource control policy (RCP).",

  d4c3: "<b>Why five services:</b> threats differ. Some arrive as web requests and some as floods of traffic. Some are already at work inside your account. Some are weak spots in your software, and some are sensitive data lying where it should not. Each service watches for one of these.<br><b>WAF:</b> filters web requests at layer 7: SQL injection, XSS, IP sets, geo match, rate-based rules, managed rule groups. It works on CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway REST APIs, AppSync, Cognito user pools and a few others (App Runner, Verified Access, Amplify).<br><b>Shield:</b> DDoS protection. Standard is free and automatic at layers 3/4. Advanced adds the Shield Response Team, enhanced detection and cost protection for CloudFront, Route 53, Global Accelerator, load balancers and Elastic IPs.<br><b>GuardDuty:</b> threat detection. It reads CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs, plus optional S3, EKS, RDS, Lambda, runtime and malware protection. It finds compromised instances, crypto mining and unusual API calls.<br><b>Inspector:</b> continuous vulnerability (CVE) and network-exposure scanning of EC2, ECR images and Lambda functions.<br><b>Macie:</b> discovers sensitive data such as PII in S3.<br><b>Pick:</b> harmful web requests: WAF. A flood of traffic: Shield. Suspicious activity in the account: GuardDuty. Vulnerable software: Inspector. Sensitive data in buckets: Macie.",

  d5c1: "<b>Why three load balancers:</b> spreading traffic over several servers means different things at different layers of the network. One load balancer reads web requests, one passes connections through as fast as possible, and one sends traffic through security appliances.<br><b>ALB (layer 7):</b> for HTTP, HTTPS and gRPC. It routes on path, host, headers and query strings. Targets are EC2, IP addresses, containers and Lambda. It supports WAF, redirects and user authentication through Cognito or OIDC.<br><b>NLB (layer 4):</b> for TCP, UDP, TLS and QUIC at millions of requests per second with very low latency. It has one static IP per AZ (Elastic IPs allowed) and preserves the client source IP. It is the load balancer behind a PrivateLink endpoint service.<br><b>Gateway Load Balancer (layer 3):</b> deploys and scales third-party virtual appliances (firewalls, IDS/IPS) transparently. It uses GENEVE on port 6081 and GWLB endpoints referenced in route tables.<br><b>Pick:</b> path-based routing: ALB. Static IP, non-HTTP or extreme throughput: NLB. Inspect all traffic with appliances: GWLB.",

  d5c2: "<b>Why four policy types:</b> an Auto Scaling group needs a rule for when to add and remove instances. The best rule depends on how the load changes: with a metric, on a calendar, or in a pattern that repeats.<br><b>Target tracking:</b> the default choice. It holds a metric at a target value, for example average CPU 50% or requests per target, like a thermostat. AWS creates and manages the alarms.<br><b>Step scaling:</b> for a response that grows with the size of the breach. You create CloudWatch alarms and define the adjustments, for example +1 instance at 70% CPU and +3 at 90%.<br><b>Scheduled:</b> for known times, such as business hours or a planned sale. It changes min, desired and max capacity at set times.<br><b>Predictive:</b> for recurring daily or weekly patterns. It analyzes past load (up to 14 days of metric history), forecasts the pattern and launches capacity before demand arrives. Pair it with target tracking for the unexpected.<br><b>Pick:</b> target tracking, unless the stem names set times (scheduled), a recurring pattern (predictive) or adjustments by size of breach (step). Instance warmup keeps new instances from skewing the metric before they are ready.",

  d5c3: "<b>Why two health checks:</b> an instance can be running while the application on it has crashed. A check that looks only at the machine misses that. The load balancer's check asks the application itself.<br><b>EC2 health checks:</b> the default for an Auto Scaling group (status checks and scheduled events). The group replaces an instance that is not running or whose system or instance status checks are impaired: hardware, network, OS. An application that has crashed on an otherwise healthy instance goes unnoticed.<br><b>ELB health checks:</b> enabled on the group. The group then also replaces instances that the load balancer marks unhealthy on its target group health check, for example no HTTP 200 from <code>/health</code>.<br><b>A load balancer on its own:</b> only stops sending traffic to an unhealthy target. It never replaces it.<br><b>Pick:</b> to have instances with a crashed application replaced, enable ELB health checks on the group. Set a health check grace period so instances are not terminated while they are still booting.",

  d6c1: "<b>Why three services:</b> running a system raises three different questions. How is it performing? Who did what? How is each resource configured, and has that changed? Each service keeps the record for one of them.<br><b>CloudWatch:</b> performance and operations: metrics, logs, alarms and dashboards. Alarms can trigger scaling, instance recovery or SNS notifications. Memory and disk metrics need the CloudWatch agent.<br><b>CloudTrail:</b> the audit of API activity: who did what, when and from where. Event history keeps 90 days of management events. A trail delivers logs to S3 for longer retention, data events and organization-wide logging.<br><b>Config:</b> inventory and history of resource configurations. It evaluates them against Config rules and conformance packs, with optional automatic remediation.<br><b>Pick:</b> by the question. Example: a security group opened to 0.0.0.0/0. Config flags it as noncompliant. CloudTrail shows who made the change. CloudWatch can alarm on a metric filter over the trail's logs.",

  d6c2: "<b>Why six pillars:</b> a design can be good in one way and poor in another, for example cheap but fragile. The Well-Architected Framework names six goals, so that each can be judged by itself.<br><b>Operational excellence:</b> run and improve workloads through infrastructure as code, small reversible changes, observability and learning from failures.<br><b>Security:</b> strong identity and least privilege, traceability, defense in depth, data protection at rest and in transit.<br><b>Reliability:</b> recover automatically from failure, scale horizontally, test recovery, manage quotas and change.<br><b>Performance efficiency:</b> pick the right resource types, use serverless and global services, experiment and review.<br><b>Cost optimization:</b> pay only for what you need, measure spending, use managed services and the right pricing models.<br><b>Sustainability:</b> reduce environmental impact by maximizing utilization and using efficient hardware (such as Graviton) and managed services.<br><b>Pick:</b> match a practice to its pillar by the goal the stem states.",

  d6c3: "<b>Why three services:</b> the producers and the consumers of data rarely work at the same speed, so something must hold the data in between. Which service depends on what the data is: a stream to read more than once, a load to deliver somewhere, or jobs to hand out one by one.<br><b>Kinesis Data Streams:</b> for replay or many readers. A real-time stream with ordering per shard (by partition key), retention from 24 hours up to 365 days, and replay. Several independent consumers read it, and you build them: Lambda, KCL apps, Managed Service for Apache Flink. Capacity is provisioned or on-demand.<br><b>Amazon Data Firehose</b> (formerly Kinesis Data Firehose): for loading data into a destination with no code. Fully managed delivery into S3, Redshift, OpenSearch, Splunk, Apache Iceberg tables or HTTP endpoints. It buffers data (near-real-time), can transform records with Lambda and convert to Parquet or ORC. No consumer code and no replay.<br><b>SQS:</b> a queue for decoupling. Each message is processed by one consumer and then deleted. Retention is up to 14 days. There is no replay and no fan-out (add SNS for fan-out).<br><b>Pick:</b> replay or many readers: Data Streams. Load into a destination with no code: Firehose. Work queue: SQS.",

  d8c1: "<b>Why three services:</b> two different groups of people sign in. Your app's customers need accounts in your app, and sometimes AWS credentials behind it. Your own staff need access to AWS accounts and business apps. Each need has its own service.<br><b>Cognito user pools:</b> authentication for your app's customers. A user directory with sign-up, sign-in, MFA and social, SAML or OIDC federation. It issues JWTs and plugs into API Gateway authorizers and ALB authentication.<br><b>Cognito identity pools:</b> authorization to AWS. They exchange a token (from a user pool, another IdP, or a guest session) for temporary AWS credentials through STS, mapped to IAM roles. The app can then call services such as S3 directly.<br><b>IAM Identity Center:</b> workforce single sign-on for employees across all accounts in AWS Organizations and for business apps. Permission sets become roles in each account. The identity source can be its own directory, Active Directory or an external IdP (SAML 2.0 with SCIM).<br><b>Pick:</b> your app's customers: Cognito. Your staff: Identity Center.",

  d8c2: "<b>Why cross-account roles:</b> someone in one account must work in another. Handing over access keys would mean sharing a long-term secret. A role in the other account gives temporary credentials instead, so no access keys are shared.<br><b>The role:</b> in the account that owns the resources, create a role. Its <b>trust policy</b> names the other account (or a specific role in it) as a principal allowed to call <code>sts:AssumeRole</code>. Its permissions policy limits what it can do.<br><b>The caller:</b> in the calling account, give the user or role an identity policy that allows <code>sts:AssumeRole</code> on that role ARN. STS returns temporary credentials.<br><b>External ID:</b> for a third party that assumes roles in many customers' accounts. Each trust policy requires a unique <code>sts:ExternalId</code> condition. This stops the confused-deputy problem, where someone tricks the vendor into using your role.<br><b>Pick:</b> a role for people or services from another account, plus an external ID when the caller is a third party. Alternatives: resource-based policies (S3, KMS, SQS) grant cross-account access directly, and the <code>aws:PrincipalOrgID</code> condition limits access to your organization.",

  d8c3: "<b>Why two kinds of encryption:</b> data can be read in two places: where it is stored and on the way between systems. Encryption at rest protects the first and TLS the second. One does not replace the other.<br><b>At rest:</b> KMS keys protect data through envelope encryption in S3, EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS and more. In S3 the default is SSE-S3 with S3-managed keys, and SSE-KMS is for key control and audit. Customer managed keys add key policies, rotation, cross-account use and CloudTrail audit. CloudHSM gives single-tenant HSMs that only you control.<br><b>An existing RDS database:</b> RDS encryption is chosen at creation. To encrypt an existing database, snapshot it, copy the snapshot with encryption and restore. A blue/green deployment with an encrypted green environment also works.<br><b>In transit:</b> TLS. ACM issues public certificates for ALB, NLB, CloudFront and API Gateway. It renews them automatically when they are DNS-validated, in use by an AWS service and their validation CNAME records are still in DNS. Certificates for CloudFront must be in us-east-1. Enforce HTTPS to S3 with an <code>aws:SecureTransport</code> condition.<br><b>WAF attaches to</b> CloudFront, ALB and API Gateway REST APIs (also AppSync, Cognito user pools and a few others), not to NLBs or directly to EC2 instances.<br><b>Pick:</b> stored data: encryption with a KMS key, customer managed when you need key policies, rotation, cross-account use or audit. Data on the way: TLS with an ACM certificate.",

  d9c1: "<b>Why two queue types:</b> a queue lets a sender carry on while the receiver is busy or down. Most work only needs every message delivered, as fast as possible. Some work breaks when a message arrives twice or out of order. Standard serves the first kind and FIFO the second.<br><b>Standard:</b> nearly unlimited throughput, at-least-once delivery (occasional duplicates), best-effort ordering.<br><b>FIFO:</b> strict ordering per message group ID and exactly-once processing through deduplication (5-minute window). 300 API calls per second per action, 3,000 messages per second with batching, higher with high-throughput mode. The queue name ends in <code>.fifo</code>.<br><b>Visibility timeout:</b> after a consumer receives a message it is hidden (default 30 seconds, max 12 hours). If it is not deleted in time it reappears and may be processed again, so set the timeout longer than the processing time.<br><b>Dead-letter queue:</b> takes the messages that keep failing. After <code>maxReceiveCount</code> failed receives, the message moves to a DLQ for inspection and later redrive.<br><b>Long polling</b> (up to 20 seconds) reduces empty receives and cost.<br><b>Pick:</b> strict order or no duplicates: FIFO. Otherwise Standard.",

  d9c2: "<b>Why four strategies:</b> recovering faster after a disaster costs more, because more has to be running before it happens. The four strategies are steps on that scale. RPO is how much data you can afford to lose. RTO is how long you can afford to be down.<br><b>Backup and restore:</b> back up data and rebuild infrastructure from code after a disaster. RPO and RTO in hours. Cheapest.<br><b>Pilot light:</b> data is continuously replicated to the DR Region and core pieces are in place, but application servers are switched off. RPO and RTO in tens of minutes while you start and scale them.<br><b>Warm standby:</b> a scaled-down but fully working copy is always running. RPO and RTO in minutes while it scales up.<br><b>Multi-site active/active:</b> full capacity serves traffic in two or more Regions. RPO and RTO near zero (real time). Highest cost.<br><b>Pick:</b> the cheapest strategy that meets both the RPO and the RTO.",

  d9c3: "<b>Why so many policies:</b> one name can have several possible answers, and Route 53 needs a rule for which one to give. Each routing policy is one such rule: by health, by share, by latency or by where the user is.<br><b>Simple:</b> one record. Multiple values come back in random order. No health checks.<br><b>Weighted:</b> split traffic by percentage for canary, blue/green or A/B tests.<br><b>Latency:</b> send users to the Region with the lowest measured latency.<br><b>Failover:</b> active-passive. The secondary answers only when the primary's health check fails.<br><b>Geolocation:</b> by the user's continent, country or US state, for localization, licensing or compliance. Add a default record.<br><b>Geoproximity:</b> by distance between users and resources, with a bias to grow or shrink each area.<br><b>Multivalue answer:</b> up to eight healthy records at random. Basic client-side spreading, not a load balancer.<br><b>IP-based:</b> answers by the client's source IP range (CIDR blocks you define).<br><b>Pick:</b> by the word in the stem: a percentage (weighted), lowest latency (latency), a standby (failover), where the user is (geolocation). Alias records point to AWS resources, even at the zone apex, with no charge for those queries.",

  d9c4: "<b>Why there are two:</b> users in several Regions want fast reads, and the data must survive the loss of a Region. Both services copy a database across Regions. They differ in the kind of database and in where writes are allowed.<br><b>Aurora Global Database:</b> relational, with a single writer. One primary Region takes writes. Read-only secondary Regions receive storage-level replication with typical lag under a second. It gives fast local reads worldwide and cross-Region DR. A secondary can be promoted within minutes (AWS quotes an RTO under a minute) with an RPO of seconds. Managed switchover handles planned moves. Write forwarding lets secondaries pass writes to the primary.<br><b>DynamoDB global tables:</b> NoSQL, multi-Region and multi-active, so you read and write in every replica Region. Replication usually completes within a second. Conflicts resolve as last writer wins by default, and a multi-Region strong consistency mode is available.<br><b>Pick:</b> relational with a single writer: Aurora Global Database. NoSQL with writes in every Region: global tables.",

  d10c1: "<b>Why there are two:</b> users far from your Region get slow answers. There are two ways to help them: keep copies of the content near them, or carry their traffic to you over the AWS network instead of the public internet. CloudFront does the first and Global Accelerator the second.<br><b>CloudFront:</b> a CDN that caches HTTP(S) content at edge locations. Best for static and cacheable dynamic web content. It has OAC for S3 origins, signed URLs and cookies, geo restriction, WAF and edge functions.<br><b>Global Accelerator:</b> no caching. It provides two static anycast IPs and carries TCP or UDP traffic from the nearest edge over the AWS backbone to the closest healthy Regional endpoint (ALB, NLB, EC2, Elastic IP). Failover does not depend on DNS caches expiring.<br><b>Pick:</b> caching, HTTP, offloading the origin: CloudFront. Static IPs, UDP for gaming, IoT or VoIP, fast multi-Region failover: Global Accelerator. Both include Shield Standard.",

  d10c2: "<b>Why three fixes:</b> a database that cannot keep up can have three different causes: too many connections, too many reads, or the same reads over and over. Each fix removes one cause.<br><b>RDS Proxy:</b> for too many connections. A managed connection pool in front of RDS or Aurora, for many short-lived connections (typically Lambda) that would otherwise exhaust the database. It also shortens failover and can enforce IAM authentication with credentials in Secrets Manager. It does not add read capacity.<br><b>Read replicas:</b> for read-heavy or reporting queries. Extra copies of the database. They are asynchronous, so reads can be slightly stale, and the app must send reads to the replica endpoint.<br><b>ElastiCache:</b> for repeated queries, computed results or session state. An in-memory cache with microsecond latency (exam items often say sub-millisecond). The app needs caching logic and a TTL or invalidation strategy.<br><b>Pick:</b> too many connections: Proxy. Heavy, varied reads: replicas. The same hot data read again and again: ElastiCache.",

  d10c3: "<b>Why so many analytics services:</b> getting answers out of data takes several steps: bring it in, prepare it, then query it. How you do each step depends on how much data there is, how often you ask and how much control you need. Each service covers one step or one style of query.<br><b>Athena:</b> for ad hoc queries. Serverless SQL directly on data in S3, priced per data scanned. Partitioning and columnar formats (Parquet, ORC) cut both cost and time.<br><b>Glue:</b> for preparing data. Serverless ETL (Spark) and the Data Catalog shared by Athena, EMR and Redshift Spectrum. Crawlers infer schemas.<br><b>EMR:</b> for big-data jobs that need framework control. Managed Spark, Hadoop, Hive, Trino/Presto and more on EC2, EKS or EMR Serverless.<br><b>Redshift:</b> for complex, repeated BI and analytics on structured data. A columnar data warehouse. Spectrum queries S3 without loading it.<br><b>Kinesis family:</b> for data that arrives as a stream. Data Streams (real-time ingest, custom consumers), Amazon Data Firehose (managed delivery to destinations), Managed Service for Apache Flink (real-time stream processing, formerly Kinesis Data Analytics), Kinesis Video Streams (video ingest).<br><b>Pick:</b> ad hoc SQL on S3: Athena. ETL and a shared catalog: Glue. Control over Spark or Hadoop: EMR. A warehouse for repeated BI: Redshift. Data arriving as a stream: the Kinesis family.",

  d11c1: "<b>Why these classes:</b> much data is kept for years and hardly ever read, and paying the Standard price for it is waste. Cheaper storage brings waits or minimum storage times. Intelligent-Tiering decides for you when you cannot predict access. The Glacier classes are for data you know is rarely read.<br><b>Intelligent-Tiering:</b> for unknown or changing access patterns. Objects move automatically between Frequent, Infrequent (after 30 days without access) and Archive Instant Access (after 90 days) tiers. Archive Access and Deep Archive Access tiers are optional, and you opt into them. Small per-object monitoring fee, no retrieval fees, no minimum storage duration. Objects under 128 KB are not tiered.<br><b>Glacier Instant Retrieval:</b> milliseconds, for data read about once a quarter. 90-day minimum.<br><b>Glacier Flexible Retrieval:</b> expedited 1–5 minutes, standard 3–5 hours, bulk 5–12 hours. 90-day minimum.<br><b>Glacier Deep Archive:</b> cheapest. Standard within 12 hours, bulk within 48 hours. 180-day minimum. Suits multi-year compliance archives.<br><b>Pick:</b> access you cannot predict: Intelligent-Tiering. Data you know is rarely read: the Glacier class whose retrieval time you can accept.",

  d11c2: "<b>Why several discounts:</b> On-Demand needs no commitment and gives no discount. AWS charges less when you commit to steady use or accept interruption. The options differ in what you promise and how much freedom you keep.<br><b>Compute Savings Plans:</b> the most flexible option. Commit to a $/hour spend for 1 or 3 years for up to 66% off. It applies automatically to any EC2 family, size, Region, OS or tenancy, plus Fargate and Lambda.<br><b>EC2 Instance Savings Plans:</b> up to 72% off, but tied to one instance family in one Region. Size, OS, tenancy and AZ can change.<br><b>Reserved Instances:</b> similar discounts tied to instance attributes. Standard RIs can be sold on the RI Marketplace, Convertible RIs can be exchanged, and zonal RIs also reserve capacity.<br><b>Databases:</b> reserved pricing also exists for RDS, ElastiCache, Redshift and OpenSearch. Database Savings Plans (up to 35% off, 1-year term, no upfront payment) cover Aurora, RDS, DynamoDB and other database services.<br><b>Spot:</b> up to 90% off, interruptible with a 2-minute notice.<br><b>Pick:</b> the typical mix is Savings Plans for the steady baseline, On-Demand for peaks, Spot for interruptible work.",

  d11c3: "<b>Why network costs matter:</b> on AWS you pay for traffic as well as for servers, and the charge depends on the path the traffic takes. Sending everything through a NAT gateway or across zones costs more than the short, private path.<br><b>NAT gateway:</b> billed per hour, plus a per-GB processing charge on all traffic through it, including traffic to S3 or DynamoDB in the same Region. A zonal NAT gateway is needed in each AZ for resilience. A regional NAT gateway spans AZs automatically.<br><b>Gateway endpoints:</b> free, for S3 and DynamoDB. Routing that traffic through them removes the NAT processing charge.<br><b>Interface endpoints:</b> cost per AZ-hour plus a per-GB fee lower than NAT, and keep traffic private.<br><b>Data transfer:</b> inbound from the internet is free. Outbound to the internet is charged per GB. Transfer from AWS origins to CloudFront is free, which helps. Traffic between AZs is charged in both directions. Traffic within one AZ over private IPs is free. Traffic between Regions is charged.<br><b>Pick:</b> S3 or DynamoDB traffic from private subnets: a gateway endpoint instead of the NAT gateway. Keep chatty components in one AZ only when availability allows, and talk over private IPs rather than public or Elastic IPs."
};

